{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02-derivations.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Typing derivations",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Say exactly where a term with no type gets stuck, read derivations in a context, and see why a term has only one type.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\Omega$'s half, $\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x$, applies $x$ to $x$, so $x$ must be a function. Try to give it a type.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> What goes wrong with $x : A$? With $x : A \\to A$? Predict each message.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: λx:A. x x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: λx:A→A. x x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A base type cannot be applied. An arrow can, but $x : A \\to A$ wants an $A$ and is handed itself. Whatever type $T$ you pick, $x\\ x$ needs $T = T \\to B$ for some $B$: $T$ would be a proper part of itself, and no type is. So $\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x$ has no type, and $\\Omega$ has none either. Lesson 4 returns to what that buys.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Reading a derivation",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A derivation is a certificate: each step can be checked on its own, against its premises. The checker builds it bottom-up: to type $\\lambda x{:}A.\\, M$ it adds $x : A$ to the context and types $M$; to type $M\\ N$ it types both and checks that they fit. The tree is read the other way, from the axioms down. Long contexts are named $\\Gamma_1, \\Gamma_2, \\ldots$ under the tree.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: λf:A→B. λg:B→C. λx:A. g (f x)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Where a derivation gets stuck",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Each rule has a condition, and a term without a type is one where some condition fails: Var, when a name has no type; →E, when the function is not a function (as with $x\\ x$ at $x : A$), or when its argument has the wrong type.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Which condition fails in each of these?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: λf:A→B. λx:B. f x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: λx:A. y",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Shadowing",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake] The outer x\n> A natural guess for $\\lambda x{:}A.\\, \\lambda x{:}B.\\, x$ is $A \\to B \\to A$, reading the last $x$ as the first binder's. But the inner binder hides the outer one: the context remembers only the nearest $x$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: λx:A. λx:B. x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "It has to be so, or the type would disagree with what the term does: given two arguments, it returns the second.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal: (λx. λx. x) a b",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "One type",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "At no node of a derivation is there a choice: the term's shape picks the rule, and the binder's type extends the context. So a term has at most one type in a context; the Lean below proves it. Take the binder's type away and the choice is back: $\\lambda x.\\, x$ could be $A \\to A$, or $B \\to B$, or $(A \\to B) \\to A \\to B$, and the checker will not guess:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: λx. x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The checker, as a function, and the proof that it is right: whatever type it returns, the term has that type by the rules. The engine's `type:` is the same checker, with the same theorem (`check_sound`), which is why its steps are marked verified.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- The checker: the type, if the term has one. -/\ndef typeOf (Γ : Ctx) : Tm → Option Ty\n  | .var x => Γ.lookup x\n  | .lam x A b => (typeOf ((x, A) :: Γ) b).map (A ⇒ ·)\n  | .app f a =>\n    match typeOf Γ f, typeOf Γ a with\n    | some (.arrow A B), some A' => if A = A' then some B else none\n    | _, _ => none\n\n#eval typeOf [] (.lam \"x\" A (.var \"x\"))\n#eval typeOf [] (.lam \"x\" A (.app (.var \"x\") (.var \"x\")))\n\n/-- What the checker says is so. -/\ntheorem typeOf_sound : ∀ (t : Tm) (Γ : Ctx) (T : Ty), typeOf Γ t = some T → HasType Γ t T := by\n  intro t\n  induction t with\n  | var x => intro Γ T h; exact .var h\n  | lam x A b ih =>\n    intro Γ T h\n    simp only [typeOf, Option.map_eq_some_iff] at h\n    obtain ⟨B, hb, rfl⟩ := h\n    exact .abs (ih _ _ hb)\n  | app f a ihf iha =>\n    intro Γ T h\n    simp only [typeOf] at h\n    split at h\n    · rename_i A B A' hf ha\n      split at h\n      · rename_i hA; subst hA; cases h; exact .app (ihf _ _ hf) (iha _ _ ha)\n      · cases h\n    · cases h",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem HasType.unique {Γ : Ctx} {t : Tm} {T U : Ty} (h₁ : HasType Γ t T) (h₂ : HasType Γ t U) : T = U := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "A term has at most one type in a context. Prove it by induction on the first derivation.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction h₁ generalizing U with\n  | var h => cases h₂ with | var h' => rw [h] at h'; exact Option.some.inj h'\n  | abs _ ih => cases h₂ with | abs h' => rw [ih h']\n  | app _ _ ihf _ => cases h₂ with | app hf' _ => cases ihf hf'; rfl",
      "hints": [
        "`induction h₁ generalizing U with`, and in each case `cases h₂`: the second derivation must end with the same rule.",
        "Var: both lookups give `some _`, so the types are equal (`Option.some.inj`). →I: the bodies' types agree by the hypothesis. →E: the functions' types `A ⇒ T` and `A' ⇒ U` agree, so `T = U`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: f : A → B, g : B → C ⊢ λx:A. g (f x)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "In the context $f : A \\to B,\\ g : B \\to C$, what type does $\\lambda x{:}A.\\, g\\ (f\\ x)$ have?",
      "hints": [
        "$f\\ x : B$, then $g$ takes it to $C$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: λx:A→B→C. λy:A→B. λz:A. x z (y z)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "What type does $\\lambda x{:}A \\to B \\to C.\\, \\lambda y{:}A \\to B.\\, \\lambda z{:}A.\\, x\\ z\\ (y\\ z)$ have? (It is $S$.)",
      "hints": [
        "The body has type $C$; put the three binders' types in front."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "type: y : A ⊢ λx:A→B. x y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "In the context $y : A$, the term $\\lambda x{:}T.\\, x\\ y$ should give back a $B$. Choose $T$, and give the type of the whole term.",
      "hints": [
        "$x$ is applied to $y : A$ and must return a $B$.",
        "So $T = A \\to B$, and the term has type $T \\to B$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A derivation is a certificate, checked rule by rule. A term has no type when a rule's condition fails: a variable with no type, a non-function applied, an argument of the wrong type; $\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x$ fails for every choice of type. An inner binder hides an outer one. The checker is sound, and a term has at most one type in a context.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Every binder so far carried its type, and without them the checker refuses. Must every type be written, or can it be worked out from how a term uses its variables?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
